1. Project title
IMDb Movie Review Sentiment Analysis Using Deep Learning
2. Problem statement
The goal of this project is to perform sentiment analysis on IMDb movie reviews using machine learning and deep learning techniques.
Sentiment analysis is a natural language processing task that identifies whether a text expresses positive or negative sentiment. This type of analysis is widely used in recommendation systems, customer feedback analysis, product review monitoring, and social media analytics.
In this project, the model predicts whether a movie review is positive or negative based on the review text. Two approaches were implemented and compared:
•	a traditional machine learning baseline model,
•	a deep learning model based on LSTM neural networks.
The project demonstrates the effectiveness of deep learning methods for text classification tasks.
3. Dataset description
The dataset used in this project is the IMDb Movie Reviews Dataset.
Dataset source: Kaggle IMDb Dataset
The dataset contains 50,000 movie reviews labeled as either positive or negative.
Dataset structure:
Feature	Description
review	Text of the movie review
sentiment	Sentiment label (positive or negative)
Dataset Statistics:
Metric	Value
Total samples	50,000
Positive reviews	25,000
Negative reviews	25,000
The dataset is balanced, making it suitable for binary sentiment classification tasks.
The reviews vary significantly in length and writing style, which creates challenges for text processing and model training.
4. Data preprocessing
Several preprocessing techniques were applied before model training.
The preprocessing pipeline included:
•	converting text to lowercase,
•	removing punctuation,
•	removing HTML tags,
•	removing stopwords,
•	tokenization,
•	duplicate removal.
The dataset was split into:
•	training set,
•	validation set,
•	test set.
The validation set was used during training and hyperparameter tuning, while the test set remained unseen until final evaluation.
For the Logistic Regression model, TF-IDF vectorization was applied to convert text into numerical features.
For the LSTM model, tokenization and sequence padding were used to prepare text sequences for neural network processing.
5. Model architecture
Two models were implemented in this project.
1. Baseline model
The baseline model used:
•	TF-IDF Vectorization,
•	Logistic Regression.
TF-IDF converts text into numerical vectors based on word importance across documents.
Logistic Regression was selected as a traditional machine learning baseline because it performs well for text classification and provides interpretable results.
2. Deep learning model
The deep learning model was implemented using an LSTM neural network.
Architecture
Embedding Layer → LSTM Layer → Dropout Layer → Dense Output Layer
Layer description
•	Embedding Layer:
Converts words into dense vector representations.
•	LSTM Layer:
Captures sequential dependencies and contextual information in text.
•	Dropout Layer:
Reduces overfitting during training.
•	Dense Output Layer:
Uses sigmoid activation for binary classification.
The LSTM model was selected because recurrent neural networks are effective for sequential text data and sentiment analysis tasks.
6. Training setup
The models were trained using the following configuration.
Parameter	Value
Optimizer	Adam
Loss Function	Binary Crossentropy
Batch Size	32
Epochs	10
Validation Set	Used
EarlyStopping	Enabled
EarlyStopping was applied to prevent overfitting and stop training when validation performance stopped improving.
The dataset was split into training, validation, and test sets to ensure fair evaluation.
7. Evaluation metrics
The following evaluation metrics were used:
•	Accuracy,
•	Precision,
•	Recall,
•	F1-score,
•	Confusion Matrix.
Accuracy: Accuracy measures the percentage of correct predictions among all predictions.
Precision: Precision measures how many predicted positive samples are actually positive.
Recall: Recall measures how many actual positive samples were correctly identified.
F1-score: F1-score balances precision and recall and is useful for binary classification tasks.
Confusion matrix: The confusion matrix helps analyze:
•	false positives,
•	false negatives,
•	classification errors.
These metrics provide a comprehensive evaluation of model performance.
8. Results table
Model comparison:
Model	Accuracy	F1-score
Logistic Regression	0.89	0.89
LSTM	0.91	0.91
The LSTM model achieved better performance than the traditional Logistic Regression baseline.
Training and validation accuracy curves showed stable learning behavior.
Loss curves demonstrated successful convergence during training without severe overfitting.
Confusion matrix analysis also showed that the LSTM model reduced classification errors compared to the baseline model.
9. Error analysis
Although the models achieved strong performance, several prediction errors were observed.
The models struggled with:
•	sarcastic reviews,
•	mixed sentiment reviews,
•	ambiguous emotional expressions,
•	short reviews with limited context.
Example: “The movie was so bad that it became funny.”
This type of sentence is difficult because sarcasm changes the actual sentiment meaning.
Some reviews also contained both positive and negative opinions, making classification more challenging.
The error analysis demonstrated that contextual understanding remains a difficult problem in sentiment analysis.
10. Limitations
This project has several limitations.
•	Limited computational resources restricted training time and model complexity.
•	The LSTM model may still struggle with sarcasm and complex linguistic patterns.
•	The project used only one dataset, which may limit generalization to other domains.
•	Hyperparameter tuning was limited due to time constraints.
More advanced transformer-based models such as BERT or DistilBERT may achieve better performance.
11. Conclusion
This project demonstrated the application of machine learning and deep learning methods for sentiment analysis on IMDb movie reviews.
A traditional machine learning baseline using TF-IDF and Logistic Regression was implemented and compared with an LSTM-based deep learning model.
The experimental results showed that the LSTM model outperformed the Logistic Regression baseline in both accuracy and F1-score.
The project also demonstrated the importance of:
•	preprocessing,
•	validation splitting,
•	evaluation metrics,
•	error analysis.
Overall, the project successfully achieved its objective of building an effective sentiment classification system using deep learning techniques.
Future work may include:
•	transformer-based architectures,
•	larger pretrained language models,
•	advanced hyperparameter optimization,
•	data augmentation techniques.
